export const MODEL_GUIDE = "## 'Analysis Findings' (lakehouse table dbo.analysis_findings)\nOne finding per notebook run and finding ID, from dbo.analysis_findings: estate pulse, blocking incidents, consumption, scenario correlation, and readiness findings, each with an ADR 0012 status.\nColumns:\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Run ID] string\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Finding ID] string: Stable finding key: analysis, target, and a per-analysis key, for example blocking.T1.20261021T1405.\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Source Notebook] string\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Captured At UTC] dateTime: When the notebook recorded the finding (UTC).\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Analysis Key] string\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Target Key] string\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Status Key] string\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Finding] string: The finding in one sentence.\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Evidence] string: The finding's evidence as redacted JSON.\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Request ID] string\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Window Start UTC] dateTime: Start of the window the finding covers (UTC).\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Window End UTC] dateTime: End of the window the finding covers (UTC).\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Findings] (Findings): Finding rows in context.\n- [Findings Needing Attention] (Findings): Finding IDs whose most recent capture has a status that needs attention (Fail, Manual action required, or Warning).\n- [Worst Finding Status Sort] (Findings): Worst status among the latest capture of each finding in context: 1 Fail, 2 Manual action required, 3 Warning; 4 to 6 need no attention.\n- [Is Latest Finding Capture] (Findings): Visual filter for the open-findings queue: 1 when the row is its finding's latest capture.\n- [Finding In Focus Window] (Findings): Visual filter for the incident page's evidence table: 1 when the finding is on the focus incident's target and overlaps its padded window.\n- [Blocking Incidents] (Findings): Blocking incidents that the notebook detected: consecutive bins with a blocked process (deadlock summaries excluded).\n\n## 'Incident Windows' (lakehouse table dbo.incident_windows)\nOne incident window from dbo.incident_windows: a recorded incident request with its expected-signal verdict, or a signal episode that NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity detected in the telemetry. Also the incident page's picker, newest first. The \"focus\" measures describe the picked incident, or the latest one in context.\nColumns:\n- 'Incident Windows'[Incident Key] string\n- 'Incident Windows'[Incident] string: The incident for the picker, for example \"2026-10-21 14:05 UTC · T1 · Blocking (detected)\", newest first.\n- 'Incident Windows'[Incident Sort] int64\n- 'Incident Windows'[Source] string: Recorded request (a notebook-dispatched incident) or Detected signal (an episode found in the telemetry).\n- 'Incident Windows'[Target Key] string\n- 'Incident Windows'[Scenario] string: The incident scenario, for recorded requests.\n- 'Incident Windows'[Signal Family] string: The incident's signal: blocking, cpu, io, or log_rate.\n- 'Incident Windows'[Request ID] string\n- 'Incident Windows'[Window Start UTC] dateTime: Start of the incident window (UTC).\n- 'Incident Windows'[Window End UTC] dateTime: End of the incident window (UTC).\n- 'Incident Windows'[Duration (min)] double: Length of the incident window in minutes.\n- 'Incident Windows'[Peak Value] double\n- 'Incident Windows'[Peak Unit] string\n- 'Incident Windows'[Correlation Status Key] string\n- 'Incident Windows'[Correlation Evidence] string: Evidence for the window as redacted JSON: the correlation details and any detected episodes.\n- 'Incident Windows'[Run ID] string\n- 'Incident Windows'[Captured At UTC] dateTime\n- 'Incident Windows'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Incidents] (Incident): Incident windows in context.\n- [In Incident Window] (Incident): TRUE when the telemetry bin in context lies inside an incident window of the targets in context.\n- [Has Incident] (Incident): 1 when there's a focus incident, 0 otherwise.\n\n## 'Scenario Runs' (lakehouse table dbo.scenario_runs)\nOne activity request that NB_DatabaseHub_LiveActivity dispatched or recorded, from dbo.scenario_runs. Runs started from PowerShell, GitHub Actions, or the business-day profile are recorded in SQL, which Fabric can't reach; Incident Windows covers them through detected signals.\nColumns:\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Request ID] string: The request's ID.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Run ID] string\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Source Notebook] string\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Requested At UTC] dateTime: When the notebook sent or recorded the request (UTC).\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Environment] string\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Target Key] string\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Profile] string: The activity profile: baseline, business-day, off, or incident:<scenario>.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Duration (min)] int64: Requested maximum duration in minutes.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Concurrency] int64: Requested sessions; empty means the scenario's default.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Intensity] int64: Requested load level; empty means the scenario's default.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Reset Before] boolean: Whether ContosoCare was reset to its seed state first.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Auto Cleanup] boolean: Whether the run stops and cleans up at its end.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Dispatch Mode] string: How the request was sent: workflow_dispatch, or manual when P9 isn't configured.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Status Key] string\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Message] string: What happened to the request.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[GitHub Run ID] int64\n- 'Scenario Runs'[GitHub Run] string: The GitHub Actions run the request started, when it was dispatched.\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Window Start UTC] dateTime: Start of the requested activity window (UTC).\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Window End UTC] dateTime: End of the requested activity window (UTC).\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Scenario Requests] (Scenarios): Activity requests that the notebook recorded, over all history.\n\n## 'Telemetry Snapshots' (lakehouse table dbo.telemetry_snapshots)\nOne value per notebook run, analysis, target, resource, instance, database, metric, and bin, from dbo.telemetry_snapshots. Overlapping notebook runs store the same bin more than once; every measure keeps only the most recent capture of each bin (Snapshot Value).\nColumns:\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Run ID] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Source Notebook] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Captured At UTC] dateTime\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Analysis Key] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Target Key] string\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Resource Type] string: Azure resource type of the target: virtualMachines (T1), machines (T2), or servers/databases (T3).\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Resource Name] string: Azure resource name of the target.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Instance Name] string: SQL Server instance name.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Database Name] string: Database name, where the analysis is per database.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Metric] string: The metric or wait category, for example AvgCPU, Processes blocked, Lock, or Buffer IO (PAGEIOLATCH).\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Bucket UTC] dateTime: Start of the telemetry bin (UTC). Empty for analyses with one row per window; the latest sample time for memory, growth, and coverage.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Value] double\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Unit] string: Unit of Value, for example percent, rows, or MB.\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Window Start UTC] dateTime\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Window End UTC] dateTime\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Snapshot Value] (Base): Base measure. Per target, analysis, metric, and bin, keeps only the most recent capture, then sums across instances and files inside that capture. Every telemetry measure builds on it.\n- [CPU (%)] (CPU): Average CPU per bin across the selected targets and period. On T3 the value is a percentage of the service tier's limit.\n- [CPU Peak (%)] (CPU): Highest per-bin CPU peak (MaxCPU) in the selection. On T3 the value is a percentage of the service tier's limit.\n- [CPU In Incident Window] (CPU): CPU (%) only for bins inside an incident window of the targets in context; blank elsewhere, so the line draws the window in indigo.\n- [Blocked Processes (peak)] (Locks and blocking): Highest number of blocked processes in any bin (gauge, maximum per bin).\n- [Active Transactions (peak)] (Locks and blocking): Highest number of open transactions in any bin (gauge, maximum per bin).\n- [Active Sessions (peak)] (Locks and blocking): Highest number of sessions in the ContosoCare database in any bin.\n- [Lock Wait (s)] (Locks and blocking): Seconds spent in the Lock wait category, summed over the selected bins (wait deltas are stored in milliseconds per bin).\n- [Deadlocks] (Locks and blocking): Deadlocks in the selected bins: per-bin deltas of the cumulative Number of Deadlocks/sec counter.\n- [Buffer IO Wait (s)] (Waits and IO): Seconds of Buffer IO page-latch waits (PAGEIOLATCH): data pages read from storage. The IO-pressure and missing-index signal.\n- [Log Rate Governor Wait (s)] (Waits and IO): Seconds of Log Rate Governor waits: Azure SQL Database's log-rate governance, the T3 log-rate scenario's signature.\n- [Wait Time (s)] (Waits and IO): Seconds of non-idle wait time in the selection, for every wait category in context.\n- [Data and Log IO (MB)] (Waits and IO): Megabytes read and written on data and log files in the selected bins.\n- [Batch Requests] (Waits and IO): Batch requests in the selected bins: per-bin deltas of the cumulative Batch Requests/sec counter. The load axis of the explorer's scatter.\n- [Signal Value] (Explorer): The signal picked in the explorer's Signal slicer (CPU, average per bin, when none is picked), in the signal's own unit and format.\n- [Signal Value In Incident Window] (Explorer): Signal Value only for bins inside an incident window of the targets in context.\n- [Signal Threshold] (Explorer): The selected signal's attention threshold for the explorer's reference line; blank when the signal has none.\n- [Signal Peak] (Explorer): Highest per-bin value of the selected signal in context.\n- [Targets Reporting] (Estate): How many of the three SQL targets sent CPU telemetry in the selected period.\n- [Telemetry Lag at Capture (min)] (Estate): Age of the target's newest telemetry sample when the notebook captured the window (estate pulse), the larger across targets.\n- [Target Attention Signals] (Estate): The text twin of the health matrix colors, so color is never the only signal: every threshold the target crossed, or None.\n- [Latest Capture UTC] (Freshness): Newest capture time in the lakehouse across snapshots, findings, and checks. Ignores the period slicer.\n- [Hours Since Latest Capture] (Freshness): Hours between the newest capture and now (UTC).\n- [History Freshness Status] (Freshness): Pass while the history is fresh, Warning after 24 hours, Manual action required with no snapshot at all.\n- [Notebook Runs Captured] (Freshness): Notebook runs that saved telemetry snapshots, over all history.\n- [Has Telemetry Bins] (Freshness): 1 when the lakehouse holds any per-bin telemetry, whatever the filters; drives every empty state.\n- [Coverage Tables (selected run)] (Coverage): Telemetry tables that each target sent, from the deployment notebook's coverage check in the validation run the readiness page selects.\n- [Coverage Last Sample Age (min)] (Coverage): Minutes between the target's newest telemetry sample and the end of the coverage window, in the selected validation run.\n- [Coverage Attention] (Coverage): Fresh, stale (older than 15 minutes, the deployment notebook's freshness rule), or not captured, with a glyph.\n\n## 'Validation Results' (lakehouse table dbo.validation_results)\nOne check per validation run and check ID, from dbo.validation_results: the Fabric-side checks of NB_DatabaseHub_Deployment, with ADR 0012 statuses. The Azure-side catalog stays in ./scripts/validate.ps1.\nColumns:\n- 'Validation Results'[Run ID] string: Validation run ID: the run's UTC start (yyyymmddThhmmssZ) and a random suffix, so sorting by it sorts by time.\n- 'Validation Results'[Source Notebook] string\n- 'Validation Results'[Check ID] string: The check's ID, for example FABRIC.P5.CAPACITY or HUB.TELEMETRY.T1.\n- 'Validation Results'[Area] string: The check's area, for example fabric, hub, or telemetry.\n- 'Validation Results'[Target Key] string\n- 'Validation Results'[Status Key] string\n- 'Validation Results'[Message] string: What the check found. Evaluation-only results keep their EVALUATION ONLY prefix (ADR 0012).\n- 'Validation Results'[Evidence] string: The check's evidence as redacted JSON.\n- 'Validation Results'[Remediation] string: The exact step that fixes the check, when it needs one.\n- 'Validation Results'[Documentation] string: Microsoft Learn or repository page for the check.\n- 'Validation Results'[Evaluation Only] boolean: True when the result comes from an evaluation-only path (ADR 0012).\n- 'Validation Results'[Checked At UTC] dateTime: When the check ran (UTC).\n- 'Validation Results'[Report Date UTC] dateTime\nMeasures:\n- [Selected Validation Run] (Readiness): The validation run the readiness page shows: the newest run in context (run IDs start with the run's UTC start, so the maximum is the latest).\n- [Is Selected Validation Run] (Readiness): Visual filter for the check queue: 1 when the row belongs to the selected validation run.\n- [Checks (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks in the selected validation run.\n- [Checks Failed (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Fail in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Needing a Manual Step (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Manual action required in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks With Warnings (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Warning in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Passed (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Pass in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Not Supported (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Not supported in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Not Selected (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks with status Not selected in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Needing Attention (selected run)] (Readiness): Checks that need attention (Fail, Manual action required, or Warning) in the selected validation run: 0 when there are none, so the strip tiles read 0; blank when no check of the run is in context.\n- [Checks Context] (Readiness): Reference line for the readiness strip: the run's check count.\n- [Readiness (%)] (Readiness): Pass share of the checks that apply and were selected (Not supported and Not selected excluded). Never turns green in the report.\n- [Worst Check Status Sort] (Readiness): Worst status of the checks in context: 1 Fail through 6 Not selected.\n- [Run Start UTC] (Readiness): First check time of the run in context (UTC); ranks the heat strip's latest six runs.\n\n## 'Analysis' (calculated)\nEvery analysis key that the notebooks write to telemetry_snapshots and analysis_findings, with its group and grain.\nColumns:\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis Key] string: Join key, the analysis value the notebooks write (for example cpu, wait_deltas, or telemetry_coverage).\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis] string: Business name of the analysis.\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis Group] string: The signal area: Estate, CPU, Locks and blocking, Throughput, Waits, IO, Memory, Storage, Readiness, Incidents, or PostgreSQL.\n- 'Analysis'[Grain] string: What one row means: per bin, per analysis window, latest sample, per incident, per agent beat, or per run.\n- 'Analysis'[Analysis Sort] int64\nRows:\n  { \"estate_pulse\", \"Estate pulse\", \"Estate\", \"Per analysis window\", 1 }\n  { \"cpu\", \"CPU\", \"CPU\", \"Per bin\", 2 }\n  { \"gauges\", \"Blocking and transaction gauges\", \"Locks and blocking\", \"Per bin\", 3 }\n  { \"sessions\", \"Active sessions\", \"Locks and blocking\", \"Per bin\", 4 }\n  { \"counter_deltas\", \"Throughput and deadlock counters\", \"Throughput\", \"Per bin\", 5 }\n  { \"wait_deltas\", \"Wait deltas\", \"Waits\", \"Per bin\", 6 }\n  { \"wait_profile\", \"Wait profile\", \"Waits\", \"Per analysis window\", 7 }\n  { \"storage_io\", \"Storage IO\", \"IO\", \"Per bin\", 8 }\n  { \"memory_clerks\", \"Memory clerks\", \"Memory\", \"Latest sample\", 9 }\n  { \"database_growth\", \"Database growth\", \"Storage\", \"Latest sample\", 10 }\n  { \"telemetry_coverage\", \"Telemetry coverage\", \"Readiness\", \"Latest sample per table\", 11 }\n  { \"telemetry_access\", \"Telemetry access\", \"Readiness\", \"Per run\", 12 }\n  { \"blocking\", \"Blocking\", \"Locks and blocking\", \"Per incident\", 13 }\n  { \"consumption\", \"Consumption\", \"CPU and waits\", \"Per analysis window\", 14 }\n  { \"correlation\", \"Scenario correlation\", \"Incidents\", \"Per scenario window\", 15 }\n  { \"real_time_dashboard\", \"Real-Time Dashboard\", \"Readiness\", \"Per run\", 16 }\n  { \"postgresql\", \"PostgreSQL (Azure Monitor)\", \"PostgreSQL\", \"Per bin\", 17 }\n  { \"database_agent\", \"Database Agent loop\", \"Incidents\", \"Per agent beat\", 18 }\n\n## 'Date' (calculated)\nUTC calendar, 2026-01-01 to 2030-12-31, marked as the date table. Every fact table relates to it through Report Date UTC, the UTC date of the row's primary time, so a midnight-spanning incident falls on its start date.\nColumns:\n- 'Date'[Date] dateTime: The UTC calendar date.\n- 'Date'[Year] int64: Calendar year.\n- 'Date'[Month Key] int64\n- 'Date'[Month] string: Month and year, for example Oct 2026, sorted by calendar order.\n- 'Date'[Day] int64: Day of the month.\n- 'Date'[Date Label] string: The date as a label, for example 21 Oct 2026.\n\n## 'Signal' (calculated)\nThe signals that the Signal explorer can show. Disconnected on purpose: it only selects which measure Signal Value returns, with its unit, format, and attention threshold.\nColumns:\n- 'Signal'[Signal Key] string: The key that Signal Value switches on.\n- 'Signal'[Signal] string: The signal's name as the explorer lists it.\n- 'Signal'[Unit] string: Unit of the signal's values: %, processes, s, deadlocks, transactions, MB, requests, or sessions.\n- 'Signal'[Signal Family] string: The incident signal family the signal belongs to: cpu, blocking, io, log_rate, or activity.\n- 'Signal'[Format String] string: Format string for Signal Value's dynamic format.\n- 'Signal'[Attention Threshold] double: Value at which a bin needs attention; 0 means no threshold line.\n- 'Signal'[Signal Sort] int64\nRows:\n  { \"cpu\", \"CPU, average per bin\", \"%\", \"cpu\", \"#,0.0\"\" %\"\"\", 80, 1 }\n  { \"cpu_peak\", \"CPU, peak per bin\", \"%\", \"cpu\", \"#,0.0\"\" %\"\"\", 80, 2 }\n  { \"blocked\", \"Blocked processes, peak per bin\", \"processes\", \"blocking\", \"#,0\", 1, 3 }\n  { \"lock_wait\", \"Lock waits per bin\", \"s\", \"blocking\", \"#,0.0\", 1, 4 }\n  { \"deadlocks\", \"Deadlocks per bin\", \"deadlocks\", \"blocking\", \"#,0\", 1, 5 }\n  { \"transactions\", \"Active transactions, peak per bin\", \"transactions\", \"blocking\", \"#,0\", 0, 6 }\n  { \"buffer_io_wait\", \"Buffer IO (PAGEIOLATCH) waits per bin\", \"s\", \"io\", \"#,0.0\", 1, 7 }\n  { \"io_mb\", \"Data and log IO per bin\", \"MB\", \"io\", \"#,0.0\", 0, 8 }\n  { \"log_rate_wait\", \"Log Rate Governor waits per bin\", \"s\", \"log_rate\", \"#,0.0\", 0.1, 9 }\n  { \"batch_requests\", \"Batch requests per bin\", \"requests\", \"activity\", \"#,0\", 0, 10 }\n  { \"sessions\", \"Active sessions in ContosoCare, peak per bin\", \"sessions\", \"activity\", \"#,0\", 0, 11 }\n\n## 'Status' (calculated)\nThe six ADR 0012 statuses, worst first: Fail, Manual action required, Warning, Pass, Not supported, and Not selected. Only the first three need attention and carry a color; the rest stay graphite.\nColumns:\n- 'Status'[Status] string: The status word, sorted by severity.\n- 'Status'[Status Short] string: Short status word for chips and tiles.\n- 'Status'[Status Sort] int64\n- 'Status'[Needs Attention] string: Yes for Fail, Manual action required, and Warning; No for the other three.\n- 'Status'[Needs Attention Flag] int64\n- 'Status'[Status Glyph] string: The status glyph that pairs with its color, so color is never the only signal: ✕ ⚑ ▲ ✓ ⊘ ○.\n- 'Status'[Status Color] string: Hex color for conditional formatting: red, cyan, or amber for attention statuses, graphite otherwise.\n- 'Status'[Status Meaning] string: What the status means (ADR 0012).\nRows:\n  { \"Fail\", \"Fail\", 1, \"Yes\", 1, \"✕\", \"#FF4D5A\", \"A selected capability is broken, or a prerequisite is missing\" }\n  { \"Manual action required\", \"Manual\", 2, \"Yes\", 1, \"⚑\", \"#8CEEEE\", \"A documented manual step is pending\" }\n  { \"Warning\", \"Warning\", 3, \"Yes\", 1, \"▲\", \"#FFB020\", \"It works, but with a risk to address\" }\n  { \"Pass\", \"Pass\", 4, \"No\", 0, \"✓\", \"#8C8C9C\", \"Functionally verified\" }\n  { \"Not supported\", \"Not supported\", 5, \"No\", 0, \"⊘\", \"#8C8C9C\", \"The docs say the capability doesn't apply\" }\n  { \"Not selected\", \"Not selected\", 6, \"No\", 0, \"○\", \"#8C8C9C\", \"The operator turned the capability off\" }\n\n## 'Target' (calculated)\nThe demo's targets and platform scopes. T1, T2, and T3 are the SQL targets; T4 and T4-replica are the PostgreSQL flexible server and its read replica, whose Azure Monitor metrics the live-activity notebook saves when the scripts pass them (ADR 0025); the other rows are the scopes that validation checks report on. Sorted in a fixed T1-to-T4 order; never colored by hue in the report.\nColumns:\n- 'Target'[Target Key] string: Join key, the target value the notebooks write (T1, T2, T3, T4, T4-replica, all, fabric, subscription, tenant, github, repo).\n- 'Target'[Target] string: Display name with the platform, for example \"T1 · SQL Server on Azure VM\".\n- 'Target'[Target Short] string: Short label for tiles, slicers, and table rows: T1, T2, T3, or the scope's name.\n- 'Target'[Target Kind] string: SQL target, PostgreSQL target, All targets, or Platform scope.\n- 'Target'[Platform] string: What the target runs on.\n- 'Target'[Target Sort] int64\nRows:\n  { \"T1\", \"T1 · SQL Server on Azure VM\", \"T1\", \"SQL target\", \"SQL Server 2022 on an Azure VM\", 1 }\n  { \"T2\", \"T2 · Arc-enabled SQL Server\", \"T2\", \"SQL target\", \"Arc-enabled SQL Server in a nested Hyper-V guest\", 2 }\n  { \"T3\", \"T3 · Azure SQL Database\", \"T3\", \"SQL target\", \"Azure SQL Database\", 3 }\n  { \"T4\", \"T4 · Azure Database for PostgreSQL\", \"T4\", \"PostgreSQL target\", \"Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server\", 4 }\n  { \"T4-replica\", \"T4 · read replica\", \"T4 replica\", \"PostgreSQL target\", \"Same-region read replica of T4, during a session\", 5 }\n  { \"all\", \"All targets\", \"All\", \"All targets\", \"Every SQL target\", 6 }\n  { \"fabric\", \"Fabric workspace\", \"Fabric\", \"Platform scope\", \"Fabric workspace and lakehouse\", 7 }\n  { \"subscription\", \"Azure subscription\", \"Subscription\", \"Platform scope\", \"Azure subscription and resource group\", 8 }\n  { \"tenant\", \"Fabric tenant\", \"Tenant\", \"Platform scope\", \"Tenant settings\", 9 }\n  { \"github\", \"GitHub\", \"GitHub\", \"Platform scope\", \"GitHub repository and Actions\", 10 }\n  { \"repo\", \"Repository\", \"Repo\", \"Platform scope\", \"Static checks\", 11 }\n\n## Relationships (many-to-one: filters flow from the dimension to the fact)\n- 'Validation Results'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Validation Results'[Status Key] -> 'Status'[Status]\n- 'Validation Results'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Status Key] -> 'Status'[Status]\n- 'Scenario Runs'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Analysis Key] -> 'Analysis'[Analysis Key]\n- 'Telemetry Snapshots'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Status Key] -> 'Status'[Status]\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Analysis Key] -> 'Analysis'[Analysis Key]\n- 'Analysis Findings'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]\n- 'Incident Windows'[Target Key] -> 'Target'[Target Key]\n- 'Incident Windows'[Correlation Status Key] -> 'Status'[Status]\n- 'Incident Windows'[Report Date UTC] -> 'Date'[Date]";
